iew_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/7cad907c5e9c5c479e50f047c6b2334e1ca1334d","node_id":"C_kwDOQDruXtoAKDdjYWQ5MDdjNWU5YzVjNDc5ZTUwZjA0N2M2YjIzMzRlMWNhMTMzNGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/9f08c5c8f3d69ad56f94e9b5a553058f4c89a673","sha":"9f08c5c8f3d69ad56f94e9b5a553058f4c89a673","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/9f08c5c8f3d69ad56f94e9b5a553058f4c89a673"}],"sha":"7cad907c5e9c5c479e50f047c6b2334e1ca1334d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/7cad907c5e9c5c479e50f047c6b2334e1ca1334d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/590602?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/willgriffin","id":590602,"login":"willgriffin","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU5MDYwMg==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/9f08c5c8f3d69ad56f94e9b5a553058f4c89a673/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T03:11:48Z","email":"willgriffin@gmail.com","name":"Will Griffin"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T03:11:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(core): one registry identity per class; route-scoped MCP catalog (#3490) (#3534)\n\n* fix(core): register a dependency class once, under its own package\n\nA consumer app that inlines a dependency registered some of its classes\ntwice: the real class under the app''s package and a manifest stub under\nthe declaring package (smrt-agents'' AgentConfig in smrt-start), so every\ngenerated surface listed the class twice.\n\n- smrt app dev: the Vite module runner reports installed dependency files\n  with a URL query (dist/x.js?v=<hash>). getPackageName rejected those\n  frames and attributed the class to the app module below them; it now\n  drops the query.\n- smrt app build: the server bundle evaluates a class before its\n  package''s __smrt-register__ when the bundler orders its chunk first.\n  A bundle-derived identity nothing confirmed is now provisional, and the\n  declaring package''s manifest adopts that same-name, same-table\n  registration instead of registering a stub beside it. Explicit\n  registration or a merged manifest confirms an identity; a same-named\n  class on another table still coexists (#3106).\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(app-mcp): build the route catalog from the allow-listed models only\n\ncreateMcpAppServer let core generate tools for every registered class and\nfiltered by its allow-list afterwards, so a duplicate tool name between\nclasses the app never publishes (a consumed package''s models) failed every\ntools/list and tools/call on the route, for every principal.\n\nMCPConfig gains an optional classNames scope (simple or qualified names);\nthe app server passes its allow-list, so the generator builds, names and\ndispatches only the published classes. A duplicate inside the allow-list\nstill fails closed. Omitting classNames keeps the existing behaviour.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* test(template-sveltekit): serve /mcp from a production bundle consuming smrt-chat\n\nPackage suites register only their own models and run unbundled, so none\ncaught that an app consuming smrt-chat failed every MCP tools/list with a\nduplicate agentconfig_list. This Vite-SSR-builds a fixture app with chat\nand smrt-agents inlined, runs the output in plain Node, and requires one\nregistry entry per inlined class, exactly the allow-listed tools, and a\nworking note_list call. smrt-chat becomes a devDependency for it.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): serve MCP tool calls for a model without a collection class\n\nMCPGenerator required a registered collection constructor and failed\nevery call for a model that declares none (\"No valid collection\nconstructor found\"), although generated REST routes already serve such\nmodels through the registry''s default collection. smrt-start''s Note is\none: once its /mcp catalog listed, note_list still failed. The generator\nnow uses ObjectRegistry.getCollection for those models.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): resolve scoped MCP tools through their exact registry key\n\nA qualified MCPConfig.classNames scope filtered generation by qualified\nidentity, but configuration, fields and methods were then looked up by\nsimple name, and handleToolCall, supportsTaskTool and createTask picked\nthe first registered class with that simple name. Scoped to ''@b:Item''\nwith ''@a:Item'' registered first, a call ran against @a''s collection.\n\nEach generated tool now records its class''s registry key; generation,\nconfiguration, custom-action metadata, dispatch, task resolution, the\ndefault-collection fallback and the tenant cache check all resolve\nthrough it. A simple name in classNames that matches more than one\nregistered class fails closed with an explicit error. Alias keys of one\nclass no longer generate its tools twice.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): bind bundled class identity to the constructor, not name and table\n\nRound-1 review (A2): adopting a bundled registration because a dependency\nmanifest declared the same simple name and table could take an\napplication''s own class (registered before the app manifest) and stamp it\nwith the dependency''s identity. The decorator path did the same in the\nother order, adopting a dependency stub in bundled output by name and\ntable.\n\nIdentity is now constructor-bound. The smrt() library build stamps each\n@smrt() class a package declares with `static __smrtPackage__` (initialized\nbefore decorators run) and publishes `stampsConstructors` in its manifest.\nThe registry reads an own stamp as the class''s explicit package at\ndecoration, so a dependency class keeps its package in any bundle and any\norder; an unstamped class never adopts a stamping package''s entry or takes\nits manifest fields by name and table, in either order. A subclass does not\ninherit the stamp. The name-and-table manifest adoption is removed;\npackages built before stamping keep the earlier decorator-side behaviour.\n\nThe plugin''s transform hook runs `pre` so it sees authored source; the two\nexisting rewrites (prepended register import, replaced shim) are\norder-independent.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(app-mcp): allow-list model routes by each constructor''s registry identity\n\nmountMcpAppRoute and createDefaultMcpAppServer hold the model\nconstructors but passed their simple names as the allow-list, so an app\nwhose Note shared a simple name with another registered class got the\nambiguous-scope error instead of its own tools. They now resolve each\nconstructor through the registry''s constructor index to its qualified\nname (an unregistered constructor still fails closed) and allow-list\nthat. MCPToolIdentity gains an optional qualifiedName so the catalog\nfilter, tenant check and task-origin check match the generating class\nexactly. A raw simple-name allowedClassNames entry that two classes share\nremains refused.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): bind STI create subtypes and every MCP class lookup to registry identity\n\nRound-2 review (B1): with same-named STI subtypes of one exposed base in\ntwo packages, a create naming the second subtype''s qualified _meta_type\nresolved inheritance, the stored variant name, the constructor and the\ncollection by simple name and could construct the first package''s\nsubtype with its fields and write policy.\n\ngetStiVariants now binds each variant to its registry entry (subtype by\nconstructor descent, or by the base''s qualified identity in the chain for\nmanifest-only entries) and create runs through that exact entry, checked\nstill registered under its key. A sweep of the remaining simple-name\nlookups resolves them through the tool''s registry key too: the emitted\nruntime''s custom actions, task actions, list ordering and STI targets,\nand the tenant-gate name set. Lookups for tools without a recorded\nregistration fail closed across every same-named class. In app-mcp the\ntenant fallback fails closed the same way, task capability is read per\nregistration, and a persisted simple-name task origin is accepted only\nwhile that name is unambiguous.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): stamp package classes at parser-provided body offsets\n\nRound-2 review (B2): package stamping rescanned source text for\n`class <Name>` and skipped strings and comments but not regex literals\nor template substitutions, so `/class Thing {/` (or a same-named class\nexpression) received the stamp while the real class stayed unstamped,\nthe manifest still claimed stampsConstructors, and the missing-body gate\ndid not fire.\n\nThe scanner now reports each class''s parser `bodyStart` (the class body''s\nopening brace, from the AST node), and stamping inserts there only. The\nfail-the-build gate stays (no offset, or an offset that is not `{`), and\nthe result is re-parsed: every @smrt() class must open with its stamp and\nthe module must contain exactly as many stamps as decorated classes.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* test(smrt-ui): stop the form-retry storage check matching the saved timestamp\n\nThe assertion looked for the PIN ''1234'' anywhere in the stored record,\nwhich also holds savedAt (Date.now()); it failed whenever the epoch\nmillisecond value contained 1234 (seen at 1791234629655). Use values a\ntimestamp or UUID cannot contain.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): keep the stamping capability on the stored manifest record\n\nPR review (T2): `stampsConstructors` lived in a separate registry set\nfilled only by seedAndRegisterManifest(). Manifests loaded through\nloadAllManifests() (explicit paths and auto-discovery) never set it, so\nan unstamped class with the same name and table could still adopt their\nstub; and nothing cleared the set, so a later legacy manifest for the\nsame package was still treated as stamping.\n\nThe capability is now read from the package''s stored manifest record\n(`packageStampsConstructors`, manifest store), so every path that stores\na manifest carries it and every path that clears or replaces the record\ndrops it. The separate set is removed.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): carry the registry key into generated MCP runtimes\n\nPR review (T1): the exact registry identity lived only in the in-process\ngenerator. runtimeToolTargets() serialized targets whose objectName is\nthe simple display name, and both generated switch implementations\n(single-file template and modular handlers) used it for getCollection,\napplyWritablePolicy, getClass and the STI create base, while task\nmetadata turned the key back into the simple name. A generator scoped to\n@pkg-b:PickedThing therefore emitted the right catalog but a server that\ndispatched by simple name to whichever PickedThing the registry found.\n\nMcpToolTarget gains an optional registryKey (the generator stores it on\nevery target it creates), task actions carry it, and both emitters use it\nfor every collection, policy, class and STI-base lookup, keeping\nobjectName for the wire contract and the tool-prefix keyed tables. The\nmodular custom-action branch resolves its class through the tool''s key\ninstead of a simple-name search. Existing emitted-runtime tests asserted\nthe old lookup names and now expect the registry key.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): hash the published (stamped) manifest in the knowledge artifact\n\nThe library build wrote the stamped manifest (stampsConstructors) to\ndist/manifest.json but hashed the unstamped object into\ndist/smrt-knowledge.json, so a clean build failed the full knowledge\nfreshness check for every stamping package (merge queue, #3534).\n\nRefs #3490","tree":{"sha":"1c4804f06c1ce3e1b71844c06785f3b36ff11401","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/trees/1c4804f06c1ce3e1b71844c06785f3b36ff11401"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/commits/9f08c5c8f3d69ad56f94e9b5a553058f4c89a673","verification":{"payload":"tree 1c4804f06c1ce3e1b71844c06785f3b36ff11401\nparent 5954c4d25c78ca66e029aeb8e5be09604c17130e\nauthor Will Griffin <willgriffin@gmail.com> 1791256308 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791256308 +0000\n\nfix(core): one registry identity per class; route-scoped MCP catalog (#3490) (#3534)\n\n* fix(core): register a dependency class once, under its own package\n\nA consumer app that inlines a dependency registered some of its classes\ntwice: the real class under the app''s package and a manifest stub under\nthe declaring package (smrt-agents'' AgentConfig in smrt-start), so every\ngenerated surface listed the class twice.\n\n- smrt app dev: the Vite module runner reports installed dependency files\n  with a URL query (dist/x.js?v=<hash>). getPackageName rejected those\n  frames and attributed the class to the app module below them; it now\n  drops the query.\n- smrt app build: the server bundle evaluates a class before its\n  package''s __smrt-register__ when the bundler orders its chunk first.\n  A bundle-derived identity nothing confirmed is now provisional, and the\n  declaring package''s manifest adopts that same-name, same-table\n  registration instead of registering a stub beside it. Explicit\n  registration or a merged manifest confirms an identity; a same-named\n  class on another table still coexists (#3106).\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(app-mcp): build the route catalog from the allow-listed models only\n\ncreateMcpAppServer let core generate tools for every registered class and\nfiltered by its allow-list afterwards, so a duplicate tool name between\nclasses the app never publishes (a consumed package''s models) failed every\ntools/list and tools/call on the route, for every principal.\n\nMCPConfig gains an optional classNames scope (simple or qualified names);\nthe app server passes its allow-list, so the generator builds, names and\ndispatches only the published classes. A duplicate inside the allow-list\nstill fails closed. Omitting classNames keeps the existing behaviour.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* test(template-sveltekit): serve /mcp from a production bundle consuming smrt-chat\n\nPackage suites register only their own models and run unbundled, so none\ncaught that an app consuming smrt-chat failed every MCP tools/list with a\nduplicate agentconfig_list. This Vite-SSR-builds a fixture app with chat\nand smrt-agents inlined, runs the output in plain Node, and requires one\nregistry entry per inlined class, exactly the allow-listed tools, and a\nworking note_list call. smrt-chat becomes a devDependency for it.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): serve MCP tool calls for a model without a collection class\n\nMCPGenerator required a registered collection constructor and failed\nevery call for a model that declares none (\"No valid collection\nconstructor found\"), although generated REST routes already serve such\nmodels through the registry''s default collection. smrt-start''s Note is\none: once its /mcp catalog listed, note_list still failed. The generator\nnow uses ObjectRegistry.getCollection for those models.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): resolve scoped MCP tools through their exact registry key\n\nA qualified MCPConfig.classNames scope filtered generation by qualified\nidentity, but configuration, fields and methods were then looked up by\nsimple name, and handleToolCall, supportsTaskTool and createTask picked\nthe first registered class with that simple name. Scoped to ''@b:Item''\nwith ''@a:Item'' registered first, a call ran against @a''s collection.\n\nEach generated tool now records its class''s registry key; generation,\nconfiguration, custom-action metadata, dispatch, task resolution, the\ndefault-collection fallback and the tenant cache check all resolve\nthrough it. A simple name in classNames that matches more than one\nregistered class fails closed with an explicit error. Alias keys of one\nclass no longer generate its tools twice.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): bind bundled class identity to the constructor, not name and table\n\nRound-1 review (A2): adopting a bundled registration because a dependency\nmanifest declared the same simple name and table could take an\napplication''s own class (registered before the app manifest) and stamp it\nwith the dependency''s identity. The decorator path did the same in the\nother order, adopting a dependency stub in bundled output by name and\ntable.\n\nIdentity is now constructor-bound. The smrt() library build stamps each\n@smrt() class a package declares with `static __smrtPackage__` (initialized\nbefore decorators run) and publishes `stampsConstructors` in its manifest.\nThe registry reads an own stamp as the class''s explicit package at\ndecoration, so a dependency class keeps its package in any bundle and any\norder; an unstamped class never adopts a stamping package''s entry or takes\nits manifest fields by name and table, in either order. A subclass does not\ninherit the stamp. The name-and-table manifest adoption is removed;\npackages built before stamping keep the earlier decorator-side behaviour.\n\nThe plugin''s transform hook runs `pre` so it sees authored source; the two\nexisting rewrites (prepended register import, replaced shim) are\norder-independent.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(app-mcp): allow-list model routes by each constructor''s registry identity\n\nmountMcpAppRoute and createDefaultMcpAppServer hold the model\nconstructors but passed their simple names as the allow-list, so an app\nwhose Note shared a simple name with another registered class got the\nambiguous-scope error instead of its own tools. They now resolve each\nconstructor through the registry''s constructor index to its qualified\nname (an unregistered constructor still fails closed) and allow-list\nthat. MCPToolIdentity gains an optional qualifiedName so the catalog\nfilter, tenant check and task-origin check match the generating class\nexactly. A raw simple-name allowedClassNames entry that two classes share\nremains refused.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): bind STI create subtypes and every MCP class lookup to registry identity\n\nRound-2 review (B1): with same-named STI subtypes of one exposed base in\ntwo packages, a create naming the second subtype''s qualified _meta_type\nresolved inheritance, the stored variant name, the constructor and the\ncollection by simple name and could construct the first package''s\nsubtype with its fields and write policy.\n\ngetStiVariants now binds each variant to its registry entry (subtype by\nconstructor descent, or by the base''s qualified identity in the chain for\nmanifest-only entries) and create runs through that exact entry, checked\nstill registered under its key. A sweep of the remaining simple-name\nlookups resolves them through the tool''s registry key too: the emitted\nruntime''s custom actions, task actions, list ordering and STI targets,\nand the tenant-gate name set. Lookups for tools without a recorded\nregistration fail closed across every same-named class. In app-mcp the\ntenant fallback fails closed the same way, task capability is read per\nregistration, and a persisted simple-name task origin is accepted only\nwhile that name is unambiguous.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): stamp package classes at parser-provided body offsets\n\nRound-2 review (B2): package stamping rescanned source text for\n`class <Name>` and skipped strings and comments but not regex literals\nor template substitutions, so `/class Thing {/` (or a same-named class\nexpression) received the stamp while the real class stayed unstamped,\nthe manifest still claimed stampsConstructors, and the missing-body gate\ndid not fire.\n\nThe scanner now reports each class''s parser `bodyStart` (the class body''s\nopening brace, from the AST node), and stamping inserts there only. The\nfail-the-build gate stays (no offset, or an offset that is not `{`), and\nthe result is re-parsed: every @smrt() class must open with its stamp and\nthe module must contain exactly as many stamps as decorated classes.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* test(smrt-ui): stop the form-retry storage check matching the saved timestamp\n\nThe assertion looked for the PIN ''1234'' anywhere in the stored record,\nwhich also holds savedAt (Date.now()); it failed whenever the epoch\nmillisecond value contained 1234 (seen at 1791234629655). Use values a\ntimestamp or UUID cannot contain.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): keep the stamping capability on the stored manifest record\n\nPR review (T2): `stampsConstructors` lived in a separate registry set\nfilled only by seedAndRegisterManifest(). Manifests loaded through\nloadAllManifests() (explicit paths and auto-discovery) never set it, so\nan unstamped class with the same name and table could still adopt their\nstub; and nothing cleared the set, so a later legacy manifest for the\nsame package was still treated as stamping.\n\nThe capability is now read from the package''s stored manifest record\n(`packageStampsConstructors`, manifest store), so every path that stores\na manifest carries it and every path that clears or replaces the record\ndrops it. The separate set is removed.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): carry the registry key into generated MCP runtimes\n\nPR review (T1): the exact registry identity lived only in the in-process\ngenerator. runtimeToolTargets() serialized targets whose objectName is\nthe simple display name, and both generated switch implementations\n(single-file template and modular handlers) used it for getCollection,\napplyWritablePolicy, getClass and the STI create base, while task\nmetadata turned the key back into the simple name. A generator scoped to\n@pkg-b:PickedThing therefore emitted the right catalog but a server that\ndispatched by simple name to whichever PickedThing the registry found.\n\nMcpToolTarget gains an optional registryKey (the generator stores it on\nevery target it creates), task actions carry it, and both emitters use it\nfor every collection, policy, class and STI-base lookup, keeping\nobjectName for the wire contract and the tool-prefix keyed tables. The\nmodular custom-action branch resolves its class through the tool''s key\ninstead of a simple-name search. Existing emitted-runtime tests asserted\nthe old lookup names and now expect the registry key.\n\nRefs #3490\n\n* fix(core): hash the published (stamped) manifest in the knowledge artifact\n\nThe library build wrote the stamped manifest (stampsConstructors) to\ndist/manifest.json but hashed the unstamped object into\ndist/smrt-knowledge.json, so a clean build failed the full knowledge\nfreshness check for every stamping package (merge queue, #3534).\n\nRefs #3490","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxGcECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+mcQAF6RiVLnydz8lX2qJKaKwOvn\nOkO22AgtquNdBhB5ldKacRFP/cs+d4zPF/Fy1FVmJN/IP66Ct/83uNS11dz6LRtB\ntuqKfqWE26A8/6ljvI5GPArjpW98KH41kfIHG2za5H7wuJzuVxmHOuBeN9hjHcTl\nDDx+fLFOGuo1ucFP3AaIGeJwnb/C/cgivIJQEwcDn5Iab7nV59RFKSAxvnWzLQOk\nU535czKSPoK5LcHjREIoKvYYmtE21yoYLJxrYTKulRkP/+WkVoz+2heKG2xQTZcI\n6EWN97wPVymkIeCOHODvk08xWi5tcb5j8Jo5XfpyLD3ZgDIERXPN/9Zig54pVjDq\nP8VEp87YHLClULMxqIJdoHTAj5b+BG2Zw1dgu2KIJ+lLexUjQyFym7WXTnc7pFOm\nHcvdl5kzEuG15c7owaGE8RjBoEgnjXzRKnCZau1ZVQw/E+KnICV0enc1Nqq22mks\nsm+5JqLUrYDRjDbZW7Ns1yoMG0Mv3dVjZ/fcTaTBqr+VhKJ0ck9xj8D/js0iKsog\nKhFJU+kqdKJqQxAEOCONTy5sP7ABFi/PObrHQ1kUBYCeJDSQfm8CZjNDy04n++Ps\nDupQNAjtn