l":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/a3d7c453c01900fd6499f54da555ea491464b052","node_id":"C_kwDOQDruXtoAKGEzZDdjNDUzYzAxOTAwZmQ2NDk5ZjU0ZGE1NTVlYTQ5MTQ2NGIwNTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d","sha":"98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d"}],"sha":"a3d7c453c01900fd6499f54da555ea491464b052","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/a3d7c453c01900fd6499f54da555ea491464b052"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/590602?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/willgriffin","id":590602,"login":"willgriffin","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU5MDYwMg==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-03T22:02:24Z","email":"willgriffin@gmail.com","name":"Will Griffin"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-03T22:02:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(core): emit virtual-module declarations outside the dev server (#3424)\n\n* fix(core): emit virtual-module declarations outside the dev server\n\nThe plugin wrote virtual-modules.d.ts only under configureServer, so a clean\nclone failed tsc on ''@happyvertical/smrt-virt-web'' until a dev server ran.\nEmit on every configResolved/buildStart scan, rewriting only on change, and\ndrop the template''s copied src/smrt-webmcp.d.ts shim.\n\nRefs #3409\n\n* test(core): prove declarations emit on config resolution alone\n\nsvelte-kit sync resolves the Vite config without building (SvelteKit 2.70+),\nso a clean clone gets virtual-modules.d.ts from sync. Add a resolveConfig-only\ntest and correct the README wording.\n\nRefs #3409\n\n* docs(core): align the sync emission note with the documented 2.69.3 minimum\n\nBoth passages describe the same svelte-kit sync behaviour; the earlier\none already named SvelteKit 2.69.3+. Addresses the Copilot thread on\nPR #3424.\n\nRefs #3409","tree":{"sha":"6aa2c41f11174e96be3b8d5c6e12368c69275df2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/trees/6aa2c41f11174e96be3b8d5c6e12368c69275df2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/commits/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d","verification":{"payload":"tree 6aa2c41f11174e96be3b8d5c6e12368c69275df2\nparent 0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24\nauthor Will Griffin <willgriffin@gmail.com> 1791064944 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791064944 +0000\n\nfix(core): emit virtual-module declarations outside the dev server (#3424)\n\n* fix(core): emit virtual-module declarations outside the dev server\n\nThe plugin wrote virtual-modules.d.ts only under configureServer, so a clean\nclone failed tsc on ''@happyvertical/smrt-virt-web'' until a dev server ran.\nEmit on every configResolved/buildStart scan, rewriting only on change, and\ndrop the template''s copied src/smrt-webmcp.d.ts shim.\n\nRefs #3409\n\n* test(core): prove declarations emit on config resolution alone\n\nsvelte-kit sync resolves the Vite config without building (SvelteKit 2.70+),\nso a clean clone gets virtual-modules.d.ts from sync. Add a resolveConfig-only\ntest and correct the README wording.\n\nRefs #3409\n\n* docs(core): align the sync emission note with the documented 2.69.3 minimum\n\nBoth passages describe the same svelte-kit sync behaviour; the earlier\none already named SvelteKit 2.69.3+. Addresses the Copilot thread on\nPR #3424.\n\nRefs #3409","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwXuACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAhfoQAEWH7r6wlPSBR8XrI1hJWTkg\nfct0W1up+wUe/cNixsXacMhGpVhSeWYbpeWl4HXP+iT+pcm2BjIomkz2t5GefGVR\nN8k3L6b+hNPxGg0tSFFsh7a8QvLS1EV9/85gj92MRNbJmqjmZAmkn0VYKY3lTh7I\nyCutC8KdXadEVQVcYT2NqI16TwEvZ+UVEMC635z2N147afZPMfk1VUl2M9xdsSqS\nzJSGaRqMx3lbFjqHz8dZUx5GR4U9BHD+QBr1AYSjaXjUWow8XgTrCJ0jiR1aJbKM\nj7IA0IHIwMOxUqwmIQoPUj291+r/kCYixYNuv4AisA/vMGvIK5SbVHbl5Hf7a6th\nGZGNhCDcNAr79f1a9wKfSDsJiBjUHMWyhQ0/+SiF886f8epf5TLkr1TYES8f0GM+\nIZ4Ad4uWEJpVU5czpTJnJEH7lCfQwgsZMxFvj849KjC5VVnjfVsRMzSTDs2REVBo\n0+7EdXchzdLBrouXb1ahAq1UZPr+8EdCr84VA7nLcffUAOmGnGr1sMleX0ydIipO\n5iLBWM3+whhfA+6ZymkHf5YVY+ZhiEjl6c3+a03GBLx/2uDyAY1PAGaZ9KaP2mng\njNhpdxefHurvHD0bELMUhJd4FAviNtryj0jPs6t19zNW4/PDJ9ZLwNvsU3Owwwii\nXgnlUWkcEH/h7q9EccNb\n=7h79\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-03T22:02:41Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d","node_id":"C_kwDOQDruXtoAKDk4Yzg3MmJlNDk1MzhlZGI5NWUyMjcwZTJmZGQ5ZWVmNTkyNjNiOGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/happyvertical/smrt/commit/0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24","sha":"0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24"}],"sha":"98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/98c872be49538edb95e2270e2fdd9eef59263b8d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/590602?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/willgriffin","id":590602,"login":"willgriffin","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU5MDYwMg==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/willgriffin","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/commits/0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-03T18:40:17Z","email":"willgriffin@gmail.com","name":"Will Griffin"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-03T18:40:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: starter-driven consumer surface — move framework glue into packages (#3367) (#3403)\n\n* feat(config): declare consumed SMRT packages in smrt.config\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* feat(core): add single Vite preset smrt() with path conventions\n\nComposes the Oxc legacy-decorator config, smrtConsumer and smrtPlugin under\n@happyvertical/smrt-core/vite. The consumer package list comes from the\nsmrt.config consumer.packages declaration or the packages option.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* feat(svelte): add ./app shell, owner setup form and diagnostics WebMCP\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* feat(app-runtime): add canonical application ID and configuration fingerprint\n\nMove resolveApplicationId() and runtimeConfigurationFingerprint() from the\ntemplate''s runtime-identity script into the root entry so the web health route\nand process managers share one byte-compatible implementation.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* feat(app-runtime): add SvelteKit entry exporting handle and init\n\nAdd the @happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit subpath: a runtime factory\nreturning a destructurable { handle, init } that composes the fail-closed\nstartup gate, URL tenant selection (selection only), the signed session with\ntenant context, and authorized-tenant locals; plus mountable health,\ndiagnostics, owner-setup load/actions, and session layout load.\n\nOwner setup re-checks loopback peer and host per request. @sveltejs/kit is an\noptional peer, externalized from the build.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): document the SvelteKit runtime entry\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(app-runtime): export the session tenant authorization rule\n\nExtract the deployed restoreSession() direct/inherited membership check into\nisSessionTenantAuthorized() so the SvelteKit entry applies the same rule.\nBehaviour of restoreSession() is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(app-runtime): verify session tenant, run resolve once, honour RLS isolation\n\nThe SvelteKit entry no longer uses createSessionHandler. Its own session step:\n- accepts a session tenant only when isSessionTenantAuthorized() passes\n  (active direct or legitimately inherited membership); any other session is\n  unauthenticated, and only verified tenants are published as tenantContext;\n- runs downstream code at most once, rethrowing its error unchanged, and\n  fails closed with 500 and cleared locals when the session layer fails;\n- forces postgresRls for database-rls isolation.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): describe verified session tenant and RLS handling\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(app-runtime): keep skip paths and classOptions inside RLS isolation\n\nUnder database-rls isolation a session skip path now skips session loading\nonly and still runs downstream inside the request RLS transaction as an\nanonymous principal. During an RLS request classOptions()/databaseConfig()\nreturn the transaction-bound request database unless the class overrides its\ndb; the session service and tenant selector always use the base config.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): document RLS skip paths and request-scoped classOptions\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(svelte): report MCP App bridge construction failure as state\n\nuseMcpApp constructed the portable bridge inside onMount without a guard,\nso a view opened top-level (no embedding host) or given a non-exact origin\ncrashed the component mount. Surface the failure through the existing\nerror state so views keep their ordinary inline fallback.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-mcp): one-call MCP app route defaults and metadata route\n\nAdd mountMcpAppRoute to ./sveltekit so a consumer''s MCP route is a single\ncall: the allow-list is the app''s explicitly declared models, principals\ncome from the SvelteKit session locals populated by smrt-users\n(principalFromSessionLocals), and every authenticated principal must be a\nhuman with an id, a tenant and every required scope. Application tool and\nresource policies compose with the default and can only narrow it.\n\nmountMcpRoute gains an optional bearer auth source that replaces the\nsession principal when it yields an adapter. mountMcpProtectedResource-\nMetadataRoute serves RFC 9728 metadata only at the advertised path, and\ncreateHostedMcpResourceAuth (./auth) builds the cached hosted adapter from\nSMRT_MCP_* environment. createDefaultMcpAppServer exposes the same\ndefaults from the root entry. The endpoint stays one stateless POST with a\nfresh protocol server per request; no OAuth authorization server is added.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(svelte): ship McpAppsBridge view shell on ./mcp-apps\n\nReplace the template overlay''s McpAppsBridge.svelte with a shipped\ncomponent on the existing @happyvertical/smrt-svelte/mcp-apps subpath. It\nowns one mount-scoped portable bridge, renders app-supplied heading,\ndescription and children, and offers the portable ui/request-display-mode\nonly when the bound host advertises the mode; host absence, refusal or a\nmissing capability keep the view inline with a status. It imports no\nserver code, credentials or OpenAI helpers (the overlay''s\nrequestOpenAiDisplayMode is replaced by the bridge''s own gated request).\n\nStrings use the smrt-svelte i18n catalog and the control is the smrt-ui\nButton. The synthetic-host browser gate covers the advertised, missing and\nrefused modes, top-level fallback, disposal and a 320px viewport.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): point MCP Apps wiring at the shipped route and view defaults\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): validate request Origin on the MCP endpoint\n\nmountMcpAppRoute resolves its principal from the cookie-backed session, so\nanother origin could drive mutating tools as the signed-in user wherever an\napp-wide CORS policy allowed the Mcp-* headers or a same-site sibling still\nreceived the Lax cookie. Streamable HTTP servers must also validate Origin.\n\nAdd checkOrigin/trustedOrigins (names as in the chat assistant routes).\nBefore bearer authentication, principal resolution and any task or tool\ndispatch, a present Origin must parse to the request URL''s own origin or a\nconfigured trusted origin (exact scheme, host, port; null, malformed and\npath-bearing origins refused); without Origin, Sec-Fetch-Site cross-site or\nsame-site is refused. Refusals are HTTP 403 with the safe JSON-RPC error\ndata { code: ''mcp_origin_denied'', retryable: false }. Clients without\nbrowser origin signals, including the smrt-app-cli bridge, are unaffected.\nOn by default for mountMcpAppRoute with an explicit opt-out; additive and\noff unless set for mountMcpRoute. Bearer requests are checked too. The\nmetadata route and deprecated REST mounts are unchanged. Same-origin uses\nevent.url; X-Forwarded-* is never read.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): note the MCP route''s default origin check\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(cli): port app identity, operation lock, writer lease and process primitives\n\nPort the template''s smrt-runtime-identity, smrt-operation-lock,\nsmrt-writer-lease, smrt-process and smrt-provider-readiness scripts into\npackages/cli/src/app with the same state files, record shapes and messages,\nso the CLI and a running application share one lock domain. Storage\nidentity comes from @happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime (new workspace\ndependency). Readiness modules resolve from the application, not the CLI.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(cli): port app export/import portability with filesystem assets\n\nPort smrt-portability.mjs and smrt-portability-assets.mjs unchanged in\nbehaviour: schemaVersion 2 bundles, non-portable secret tables/columns\nemptied, no-replace mode-0600 publish, private regular-file bundle reads,\nexact schema match, empty-target import, parent-first plan, and the asset\nstaging journal with crash recovery. The template''s asset portability\nsuite is ported verbatim against the new modules.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(cli): add smrt app command group for application operations\n\n`smrt app install|setup|recover|start|stop|doctor|open|backup|export|import`\nports scripts/smrt-app.mjs; `migrate`, `worker [task|schedule]`, and\n`dev|build|vite` port smrt-prepare-migration, smrt-worker, smrt-vite and\nsmrt-mcp-apps. JSON output, exit codes, state files and environment\nvariables are unchanged; failures are one secret-redacted stderr envelope\nthat also carries a LocalRuntimeError code (migration_failed). `start`\nspawns bin/smrt-web.mjs so the recorded process identity proof is\nunchanged. `main()` dispatches `app` before manifest/config loading so\nVite arguments pass through verbatim. The primitives are exported without\nside effects from @happyvertical/smrt-cli/app.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): run the built CLI in-process from bin/smrt.js\n\nThe bin wrapper spawned dist/index.js with spawnSync, so a signal sent to\nthe wrapper (a container''s SIGTERM to PID 1) never reached the command and\n`smrt app worker` could not shut down gracefully the way the template''s\ndirectly-run worker script did. Import dist in-process instead; the\nsource/tsx fallback keeps the child process.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* docs(cli): document the smrt app command group\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): serialize stale lock and lease reclamation\n\nDeciding operation.lock/writer.lease is stale and unlinking it were two\nsteps, so a reclaimer paused between them could unlink the live lock a\nfaster reclaimer had just created and admit two owners (review R1; same\nlogic as the template''s smrt-operation-lock/smrt-writer-lease scripts).\nReclaim now runs under an exclusive SQLite transaction on a mutex database\nin the state directory (a kernel lock released when its holder dies) and\nonly removes the exact dead record it re-reads there. Acquisition and the\nrecord formats are unchanged.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): resolve readiness modules with ESM import conditions\n\ncreateRequire().resolve() applied require conditions and rejected\npackages that export only an `import` condition, which the template''s\nin-app import() loaded (review R3). Resolve the application''s installed\npackage through its exports map with node/import/module-sync/default\nconditions and subpath patterns.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): match the web process by exact argv tokens\n\nThe identity check accepted any command line containing `smrt-web.mjs`\nand `--smrt-instance=<nonce>` as substrings, so a recycled pid running\ne.g. evil-smrt-web.mjs or a suffixed nonce could be signalled (review R5;\nsame check as the template''s smrt-process.mjs). Require the final token to\nbe exactly the instance flag and the token before it to have basename\nsmrt-web.mjs, and expose verifyOwnedProcess() for re-checking before a\nsignal. The template launcher path still matches.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): confirm a failed start''s launcher exited before dropping it\n\nOn a readiness failure start sent SIGTERM and immediately removed app.pid,\nso a launcher that ignored SIGTERM kept serving and holding writer.lease\nwith nothing left for `smrt app stop` to find (review R4; same as the\ntemplate''s smrt-app.mjs). Escalate SIGTERM then SIGKILL through the owned\nchild handle, remove the record only after a confirmed exit, and otherwise\nkeep it and name the pid. `stop` now re-verifies the process identity\nimmediately before signalling (review R5).\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): require bundle evidence before recovering an import as complete\n\nA leftover asset journal plus matching per-table row counts was taken as\nproof the import had committed, so count-preserving modified rows were\nreported as a successful import (review R6; same as the template''s\nportability scripts). Record an additive `committedAt` in the journal\nafter the transaction commits, and otherwise accept `complete` only when\nevery table''s rows equal the bundle''s. Journals written by the template\nscripts remain readable and recover through row verification.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(cli): cover review findings R1-R6 and document the changed contracts\n\nDeterministic interleavings for stale reclamation, an import-only\nreadiness package, a launcher that ignores SIGTERM or cannot be killed,\ndeceptive process command lines, count-preserving import recovery, and a\nreal `smrt db:migrate` child with sentinel credentials (R2 counter-evidence).\nExport the new primitives from @happyvertical/smrt-cli/app.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): keep the process record when its identity cannot be re-checked\n\n`stop` treated a failed or empty re-check of the recorded pid''s command\nline as \"not ours\", so a transient ps/PowerShell failure removed app.pid\nand reported success while the writer kept running (review T1). Re-checks\nnow distinguish gone, mismatched (recycled pid), and unverifiable; only the\nfirst two drop the record, and an unverifiable process is neither signalled\nnor forgotten. An empty command line is unverifiable in readOwnedProcess\ntoo.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): resolve readiness packages with Node''s own ESM resolver\n\nThe hand-written exports resolver picked the first inserted pattern key\ninstead of Node''s PATTERN_KEY_COMPARE order and fell through a matched\nnull condition (review T2). Delegate to Node''s default ESM resolver,\nre-parented to the application root through a temporary\nmodule.registerHooks resolve hook (the parent argument of\nimport.meta.resolve is still flagged on Node 26), and keep the\ninstalled-package containment check. resolvePackageEntry is removed.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(cli): cover review findings T1 and T2\n\nA failing second process query during `smrt app stop`, and exports\nfixtures for pattern precedence, matched null targets, condition order,\nexact-before-pattern keys, array fallbacks, and invalid targets.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(chat): store clientRequestId and verified attachments on user messages\n\nChatService.sendMessage gains two optional, additive inputs: a sending\nclient''s clientRequestId (stored in metadata, used as a retry key) and\nalready-verified attachment records (stored in attachments). Both are\nuntrusted data on the actor''s own message, never authority.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* feat(chat): mount the AssistantDock server routes from one call\n\nAdd the @happyvertical/smrt-chat/sveltekit server subpath with\nmountAssistantRoutes(options), returning GET/POST handlers for a single\n[...path] route that serve everything the dock calls: member-scoped thread\nand message reads, thread creation, streamed sends, browser-tool resume,\nhost-backed attachment upload, and data-surface action preview/apply.\n\nThe principal comes from event.locals (smrt-users session handler) or an\ninjected resolver, never from headers or the body. Threads are served only\nfrom the actor''s keyed assistant session room in the active tenant. The AI\nclient is injected; allowedTools is fail-closed and every tool runs under\nexecuteAsPrincipal. Repeated clientRequestIds never store a second message.\n\nThe browser half ships from ./svelte: createAssistantHttpTransport\n(createSmrtAssistantTransport with the writeEndpoint filled in) and\ncreateAssistantHttpActionClient (HTTP AssistantActionClient with #2990\noutcome mapping).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document the mounted assistant routes\n\nDescribe mountAssistantRoutes and its browser half in the AssistantDock\nguide, README and package AGENTS, and close the guide''s gaps 1 and 7\n(no shipped HTTP action client or member-scoped read endpoint).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): reserve client requests on a deterministic message id\n\nA user message sent with a clientRequestId now gets a UUIDv5 primary key\nover tenant, room, thread, actor and clientRequestId (clientRequestMessageId)\nand is inserted, never upserted. The primary key''s uniqueness is the atomic,\ndurable reservation: a second insert, from any replica and at any later\ntime, throws ChatClientRequestConflictError before any stats update. On a\nPostgreSQL transaction handle the insert runs under a savepoint, so a\nconflict does not abort the caller''s transaction.\n\nAdd getThreadMessagesFrom (membership-gated read of a message and what\nfollows it, by id) and recordClientRequestOutcome (the author records how\nthe turn ended in metadata.turnOutcome).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): answer duplicate assistant sends from the database reservation\n\nReplace the per-process in-flight map and the 200-message transcript scan\nwith the deterministic user-message id: a send looks its reservation up by\nid, and a send that loses the insert race on any replica is answered as the\nduplicate. The answer follows the stored send: completed (a reply follows),\ncancelled, in_progress, or 409 turn_failed when its turn failed or it has\nno reply and no outcome after abandonedTurnMs. A retry never re-runs a\nturn. Each turn records its outcome on its send.\n\nThe reservation includes the thread, so one clientRequestId sent to two of\nthe actor''s threads is two sends (review F2).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): describe the durable send reservation\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): link turn replies to their send and keep it in continuations\n\nrunAssistantTurn takes an originMessageId: every reply and tool message it\nauthors carries replyToMessageId = that id (sendAgentReply gains the\noptional, room/tenant-validated replyToMessageId), and a suspension stores\nit in the continuation. A resume restores it from the consumed\ncontinuation, never from the request, reports it through onResumed and\nin its result. AssistantContinuationStore gains an optional has(); both\nbuilt-in stores implement it.\n\nChatService.getThreadMessageReplies replaces getThreadMessagesFrom: it\nreturns a stored message and the replies linked to it, not what follows it\nin the thread. recordClientRequestOutcome records the continuation a\nsuspended turn waits on and accepts a running outcome.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): settle assistant sends from their own continuation and replies\n\nDuplicate answers read a send''s replies by their replyToMessageId link, so\noverlapping sends in one thread keep their own replies (review M3). A\nsuspended send stays in_progress exactly while its continuation is\nwaiting, regardless of abandonedTurnMs (M1). A resume settles only the send\nrecorded in the continuation it consumes; the request''s clientRequestId is\nignored, and a missing or expired continuation changes no send (M2). A\nresumed leg records running while it works. Tests cover these and a send\nthat fails after its reservation is stored.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document reply links and resume settlement\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): have the turn runner record its own lifecycle, compare-and-set\n\nrunAssistantTurn reports each lifecycle fact through onState at the moment\nit becomes true, whoever reads the events: running when a leg starts (a\nresume once it consumed its continuation), suspended with the continuation\nid once it is stored and before client_tool_calls is emitted, and\ncompleted (after the reply is stored), cancelled or failed when the leg\nends. A resume whose continuation is missing reports nothing. onResumed is\nremoved (it never shipped).\n\nChatService.recordClientRequestOutcome is now monotonic per leg and\ncompare-and-set on the row revision, re-checking after a conflict: a late\nwrite from an earlier leg, a running that does not consume the waiting\ncontinuation, or anything after a terminal outcome is refused. It returns\nwhether the write applied.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): settle assistant sends from the turn, not the HTTP reader\n\nThe routes pump every turn to its end into a buffer the response reads\nfrom, and record outcomes from the runner''s onState, so a reader that\npauses, cancels or leaves early can no longer leave a send unsettled or\noverwrite a resumed leg with a stale suspended. A client disconnect still\ncancels the turn through the request signal, as the dock''s Stop does. A\nlinked assistant reply is completion evidence for duplicate answers even\nwhen the marker is stale.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document runner-recorded send outcomes and disconnects\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* test(chat): pin forward-only outcome writes on PostgreSQL\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): claim continuations on take until the resume is recorded\n\nThe built-in continuation stores'' take now claims the entry instead of\ndeleting it: no second take returns it (exactly one resume still wins) and\nhas() stays true, so a duplicate-send poll never sees a resuming send as\nsuspended with no continuation. The runner calls the new optional\nrelease() once it has recorded running; an unreleased claim expires after\nCONTINUATION_CLAIM_TTL_MS (60 s). createAssistantTurnResponse gains an\noptional onCancel, called synchronously when the reader cancels the body.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): cancel the detached turn when the response body is cancelled\n\nEach turn runs on an AbortController composed with the request''s signal and\naborted from the response''s onCancel, so a client that leaves cancels the\nturn (a pending server tool call is skipped and the send records\ncancelled) even on adapters that never abort request.signal.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): describe body-cancel disconnects and claimed continuations\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): keep resume claims alive and never run an unrecorded resume\n\nQ1: an actively claimed continuation now lives CONTINUATION_CLAIM_TTL_MS\nfrom its claim, whatever its original lifetime; the original lifetime\napplies only to unclaimed (or lapsed-claim) entries, which become\nclaimable again.\n\nQ2: onState''s result now matters for a resumed leg''s running report: when\nit resolves false or throws, the runner keeps the claim, does not release,\nand stops the leg with resume_not_recorded before any model or tool call.\nThe route returns recordClientRequestOutcome''s result.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* feat(config): add ai block to the smrt.config schema\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* feat(config): add one AI provider resolver\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* build(chat): depend on smrt-config for the shared AI resolver\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* feat(core,chat,agents): resolve AI providers through smrt-config\n\nChat dev routes, core and agents share the resolver and provider key map;\ncore also honours the smrt.config ai block.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* docs(config): document the AI provider resolver and ai block\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): bind AI credentials to their provider and redact URLs to origin\n\nCredentials follow the provider of their source; displayed base URLs show\nonly the origin; core consumes the full resolved provider when the ai block\nnames one and ignores partial blocks.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): keep AI credential binding across config layers and core paths\n\nMerge ai block layers with provider ownership, build the core client from the\nbound resolver result on every path, and normalise provider/model aliases.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(core): type the defaultModel read in AI resolution\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): route every AI config merge through one canonicalising helper\n\nFold provider/type and model/defaultModel before merging, drop a lower\nlayer''s credentials when a higher layer names another provider, and set both\nclient aliases from the resolved result.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* refactor(app-runtime): own app identity, state custody, lock, lease, and readiness\n\nThe CLI''s `smrt app` port (#3371) and the SvelteKit runtime entry (#3369)\neach carried an implementation of `resolveApplicationId` and\n`runtimeConfigurationFingerprint`, and the template server could only reach\nthe writer lease and provider-readiness probe through the CLI. Make\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime` the single owner:\n\n- the CLI re-exports app-runtime''s identity and fingerprint (one function,\n  asserted identical, golden vectors unchanged);\n- state root, operation lock, writer lease, stale reclaim, and provider\n  readiness move into app-runtime''s root entry; `@happyvertical/smrt-cli/app`\n  re-exports them unchanged, so a web process takes the same lease without a\n  runtime dependency on the CLI. File names, record formats, and messages are\n  unchanged;\n- their tests (including review findings R1, R3, T2) move with them, because\n  the `node:fs` interleaving hooks cannot reach an externalized dependency.\n\n`runtimeConfigurationFingerprint` now accepts the structural\n`FingerprintRuntime` the CLI already exported (a widening). An explicit\n`SMRT_APP_ID` no longer also requires a readable package name in the CLI,\nmatching what the web process already did.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(smrt-svelte): include MCP Apps bridge strings in the server i18n snapshot\n\n`buildI18nSnapshot` registers smrt-svelte''s catalogs by side-effect import,\nbut the shipped `McpAppsBridge` catalog (`ui.mcp_apps.*`) was never imported\nthere, so a server-built snapshot omitted its three keys and the bridge fell\nback to unresolved defaults on the client.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(smrt-svelte): export the diagnostics WebMCP contract without Svelte\n\nThe runtime diagnostics route lists `smrt.runtime.diagnostics.read` in its\npublic tool inventory, but the tool name was only reachable through the\n`./app` barrel, which loads Svelte components and theme CSS. Add the\nadditive `./app/runtime-diagnostics` subpath for the plain-TypeScript module\nso server routes import the one constant the browser tool registers under.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): honour PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH in the M5 gate\n\nEvery other Playwright config in the repository launches an installed\nChromium when this variable is set; the M5 gate''s did not, so `pnpm test:m5`\ncould not launch a browser on hosts where the downloaded headless shell is\nmissing system libraries (for example `libgbm` on NixOS).\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(core): dedupe @sveltejs/kit in the smrt() Vite preset\n\nSvelteKit recognizes `redirect()` and `fail()` results by class identity.\nA registry install resolves the SMRT SvelteKit entries'' optional\n`@sveltejs/kit` peer from the application, but a linked or workspace package\n(the M5 reference gate, a monorepo app) resolves it from its own directory.\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit`''s owner-setup redirect then\nthrows a foreign `Redirect` and a successful claim renders as a 500. The\npreset now adds `resolve.dedupe: [''@sveltejs/kit'']` through a\n`smrt:shared-runtime` plugin, a no-op for registry installs.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* fix(app-runtime): leave the provider-readiness import to Node at runtime\n\nThe readiness module specifier is operator configuration resolved from the\napplication root when the probe runs. Now that the web server imports the\nprobe, Vite analyzed that dynamic import and warned on every dev start; mark\nit `@vite-ignore` as the resolver import beside it already is.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): configure Vite with the smrt() preset\n\n`vite.config.ts` becomes `plugins: [sveltekit(), smrt()]`. The decorator\ntransform comes from the preset, and the consumed package list moves to\n`smrt.config.ts` `consumer.packages` (profiles, tenancy, users), so it stays\nexplicit and deterministic. Verified against the template''s real\n`smrt.config.ts` at Vite config time and by a full SvelteKit `vite build` in\nthe M5 gate.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): mount the SvelteKit runtime from app-runtime\n\nThe server bootstrap the template copied into every app now comes from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit`. `src/lib/server/smrt.ts` builds\none `createSmrtSvelteKitRuntime()` with the writer lease and provider\nreadiness probe from app-runtime''s root entry, so the web process no longer\nimports `scripts/` or the CLI. `hooks.server.ts` re-exports its `handle` and\n`init`; `App.Locals` extends `SmrtRuntimeLocals`; health, diagnostics,\nlayout-session, and owner-setup routes mount the package handlers, with the\nonboarding handoff cleanup as the app''s `onOwnerClaimed` hook.\n`getCollection`/`getSmrtConfig` read `runtime.classOptions()` per request.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/server/tenancy.ts`, `application-runtime.ts`,\n`runtime-diagnostics.ts` and their template tests. Their behaviour is\nproven in app-runtime''s `sveltekit.test.ts`. The M5 gate now takes its\n`diagnostics-authorization` case from that suite and keeps exercising the\nmounted route in the browser. `runtimeEntry.test.ts` proves the template\nmounts the runtime and its handlers.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): use the shipped app shell and owner setup UI\n\nThe root layout is `AppShell` from `@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/app` (Provider,\nThemeProvider with its CSS, AdminShell, TenantNav, and the read-only\nruntime-diagnostics WebMCP tool for signed-in sessions); the app supplies\nonly its navigation, title, and tenant label. `/setup` renders\n`OwnerSetupForm` with SvelteKit''s `enhance`, and `/settings` renders\n`ShellSettingsPage`.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/RuntimeDiagnosticsWebMcp.svelte` and\n`src/lib/runtime-diagnostics-webmcp.ts`. The library''s theme CSS imports\nand the `enhance` hook are proven in a real SvelteKit build and browser by\nthe M5 gate (owner onboarding, discovery, lifecycle).\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): run lifecycle operations through smrt app\n\nEvery lifecycle script is now a one-line `smrt app <operation>` call from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-cli`: `dev`, `build`, `preview` (`start`), `db:migrate`\n(`vite build && smrt app migrate`), `app:*`, and the task/schedule workers.\nCompose workers run `pnpm exec smrt app worker task|schedule`, and the\nruntime image no longer copies `scripts/`. `smrt.config.ts` takes its\napplication ID from app-runtime.\n\nDeleted: the whole `template/scripts/` directory (13 files, about 2,990\nlines) and `runtimePortabilityAssets.test.ts`, which the CLI carries verbatim\nin `src/app/__tests__/portability-assets.test.ts`. The M5 gate now takes its\ntwo asset-portability cases from that suite. `runtimeOperations.test.ts`\nasserts behaviour, not script source: every `app:*` script maps onto a real\n`smrt app` operation, the setup recovery sequence names existing scripts,\nand the deployed web/worker topology still holds.\n\nThe M5 harness runs the copied app''s own `smrt app setup` / `smrt app dev`\nand takes identity and state-root values from app-runtime. Its scope and\n`.bin` directories are now real directories of per-entry links, so `pnpm\nbuild` inside the copy (which ships `pnpm-workspace.yaml`) can never relink\nthis checkout''s `node_modules`.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* docs(template-sveltekit): describe the shipped shell and diagnostics tool\n\nThe package guide still described a hand-wired Provider/ThemeProvider/\nAdminShell layout and the template-owned `RuntimeDiagnosticsWebMcp`. Point it\nat `AppShell`, `OwnerSetupForm`, and `ShellSettingsPage` from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/app`, and at the mounted diagnostics handler.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): mount the MCP Apps overlay on app-mcp defaults\n\nThe opt-in `mcp-apps-template/` overlay is now one route file:\n`mountMcpAppRoute({ models: [Item], requiredScopes: [''items.read''], ... })`\nwith the session principal default, the default-on Origin check, and hosted\nbearer auth from `createHostedMcpResourceAuth` (profile from the runtime,\nthe application-owned `resolveHostedMcpPrincipal` binding). The metadata\nroute is `mountMcpProtectedResourceMetadataRoute`. The workflow tool and the\n`ui://` resource with its OpenAI display metadata stay as app content in the\nroute. `McpAppsBridge` comes from `@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/mcp-apps`.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/server/mcp.ts`, `mcp-hosted.ts`, `src/lib/McpAppsBridge.svelte`,\nand `mcpHostedCache.test.ts`, whose caching/retry behaviour app-mcp''s\n`auth-hosted.test.ts` covers. `mcpAppsOptIn.test.ts` pins the overlay''s file\nset and options. `pnpm typecheck` now type-checks the overlay against the\ntemplate; app-mcp and mcp-openai are workspace devDependencies for that. The\nsmrt-app-mcp README example no longer imports the deleted\n`application-runtime` module.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): ship a typecheck script in the generated app\n\nStandards §10 requires scaffolded SvelteKit templates to ship `typecheck` as\na TypeScript pass plus `svelte-check`; the template shipped only `check`.\nAdd `svelte-kit sync && tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json && svelte-check`.\nBoth halves pass in a scaffolded copy of this template, the MCP Apps\noverlay included.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(app-mcp): limit MCP catalogs by tool effect\n\nA required scope applies uniformly to every published tool, so a principal\nholding only items.read could discover and invoke a model''s create, update\nand delete tools. Add an effects option to createMcpAppServer (and so to\ncreateDefaultMcpAppServer and mountMcpAppRoute) using the WebMCP vocabulary\nread/write/destructive. mcpToolEffect classifies with the existing read-only\ndetection (canonical readOnlyHint, else the _list/_get name rule); any other\ntool is destructive unless it declares destructiveHint: false. Excluded\ntools are filtered from the catalog itself, so they are absent from\ntools/list and a direct call gets the same unknown-tool error as a\nnonexistent name. Omitting the option keeps today''s catalog.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-mcp): bind bearer principals around MCP dispatch\n\nBearer authentication runs inside mountMcpRoute, after a runtime entry has\nalready opened the request permission context (under database-rls, an RLS\ntransaction from the cookie session, anonymous without one). Add an\nadditive bindPrincipal option (also on mountMcpAppRoute): after bearer\nauthentication the smrtOptions database thunk, task handling and tool\nexecution run inside the application''s binding for that principal, and the\nresponse is materialized before the binding returns. A binder that throws\nbefore dispatch or never runs it answers 403 with the safe\nmcp_tool_access_denied error. The cookie-session path is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-runtime): run a route-verified principal in its own permission context\n\nAdd runtime.runAsPrincipal(principal, fn) for principals a route\nauthenticates itself (an MCP bearer token). It resolves the user''s live\npermissions in the principal''s tenant on the base connection, requires an\nauthorized membership, caps the set to the principal''s granted scopes, and\nruns fn in withPrincipalPermissionContext under the same RLS rule as the\nsession step, so databaseConfig()/classOptions() return a transaction that\npublishes that user, tenant and permission set. Application isolation keeps\nthe base connection. Additive; existing request handling is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): keep remote MCP read-only and bind bearer identity\n\nThe MCP Apps overlay gated every Item tool on items.read, exposing create,\nupdate and delete to read-scoped principals, and its bearer calls ran in\nthe anonymous request RLS transaction. Set effects: [''read''] and\nbindPrincipal: runtime.runAsPrincipal. A live PostgreSQL test drives a\ncookie-free bearer request through the real runtime.handle under\ndatabase-rls and asserts the bearer user, tenant and token-capped\npermissions on the transaction, the tenant''s row, precedence over another\nuser''s cookie, fail-closed denial without membership, and unchanged\napplication isolation.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): describe effect limits and bearer principal binding\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-runtime): export the bound-principal type from the SvelteKit entry\n\nCallers of runtime.runAsPrincipal() could not name the principal type\nbecause only runtime.ts declared it.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): authorize bound bearer calls with live-capped scopes\n\nrunAsPrincipal capped the published permissions to live membership, but\nthe MCP tool policy still authorized from the token''s scopes, so under\napplication isolation a member whose role no longer granted items.read\ncould read through a token that still carried it. runAsPrincipal now hands\nits callback the principal with scopes set to that effective set (additive:\nzero-argument callbacks still work), and the binder''s run() accepts it.\nDispatch authorizes with the token scopes intersected with the bound\nscopes, so a binder can only narrow authority; changing id or tenant, or\nmalformed scopes, is the safe denial. A partially revoked principal keeps\nits remaining tools. Live PostgreSQL tests cover cloud and self-hosted.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): apply effect limits to MCP task lifecycle calls\n\ntasks/get, tasks/update and tasks/cancel checked only task owner and\ntenant, so a read-only route could read, resume or cancel a task created by\na destructive task tool through an unrestricted route sharing the store.\nRecover the task''s originating action from its job row (persisted\nobject_type and method, via the public SmrtJobCollection API, no schema\nchange) and require that the allow-listed generated tool for it passes the\nroute''s effects filter; otherwise answer with unknown-task semantics.\n\ntoolPolicy is not re-applied to lifecycle calls: the job does not record a\ncreating workflow tool, and workflows (the Iolaus conformance reference)\nfront a policy-hidden generated task tool, so doing so broke a documented\npattern. Owner and tenant scoping are unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(app-mcp): document live-capped bearer scopes and task effect checks\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): gate task lifecycle calls on the effective principal\n\nA bearer whose live permissions were revoked (the binder hands back empty\neffective scopes) could still tasks/get, tasks/update (supplying the\nawaited input, so destructive work resumed) and tasks/cancel an existing\ntask: lifecycle calls skip toolPolicy and the effective-scope check ran\nonly for tool calls. Add taskPrincipalPolicy to createMcpAppServer, a\nprincipal-level predicate evaluated before the task store is touched and\nanswered as an unknown task on denial or error. createDefaultMcpAppServer\n(and so mountMcpAppRoute) supplies its principal scope policy — kind, id,\ntenant and every required scope of the effective principal — composed with\nany app predicate, which can only narrow. It needs no originating tool, so\nworkflow-created tasks behind policy-hidden tools stay reachable to an\nauthorized owner. Without the hook a custom server is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(app-mcp): document principal gating of task lifecycle calls\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(cli): use the s-m-r-t brand prose in the app command section\n\nThe README check (scripts/check-readmes.mjs) rejects the obsolete\nspelling; the pre-push hook caught it.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(svelte): annotate the owner setup form''s structural native elements\n\nThe strict raw-primitive check flags the native <form> and the hidden\ntoken <input>. Both are deliberate: the form must POST without\nJavaScript, and the hidden input is a non-interactive carrier for the\nsingle-use bootstrap token. Annotate them with the documented\nraw-primitive-allow reason instead of wrapping them in primitives.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(svelte): document AppShell props and use the emitted font-family token\n\nThe pre-push jsdoc check requires one sentence per exported prop in a\nstrict package, and the typography/token checks reject\n--smrt-typography-body-font-family, which no theme emits; the themes\nemit --smrt-font-family.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(svelte): use the emitted spacing token in McpAppsBridge\n\n--smrt-space-3 is not emitted by any theme; the emitted token is\n--smrt-spacing-3. Caught by the pre-push svelte-token check.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* test(template-sveltekit): run the bearer RLS fixture under the CI role model\n\nIn CI the PostgreSQL suites connect as the unprivileged smrt_ci role,\nwhich owns the fixture table it creates, so FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY\nrefused the fixture''s own seed INSERT (42501); locally it passed only as a\nsuperuser. Seed the rows before enabling RLS, confine all committed DDL\n(users tables, policy helpers, RLS table and policy) to a fixture schema\nreached through the connection search_path and dropped on teardown, and\ndrop the login role without DROP OWNED BY (smrt_ci administers but lacks\nthe privileges of roles it creates). Assert the application connects as a\nNOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS role that sees no rows without a principal. No\nsuperuser URL is used.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): skip only an absent generated register module\n\nThe registration fallback swallowed every ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND (and any\nerror whose message mentioned smrt-register.js), so a missing dependency\nimported by an existing generated module let startup continue without\npackage-qualified registration. Skip the import only when the error''s\nmissing module (Node''s url, or \"Cannot find module ''<path>''\" from Node and\nVite''s module runner) is the generated register module itself; rethrow\neverything else. Tests evaluate the shipped predicate against real Node and\nVite errors for both cases.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(cli): accept only task or schedule for smrt app worker\n\nrunWorker branched only on ''schedule'', so a misspelled kind such as\n''scheduel'' was reported as that kind but started the task worker. Parse the\nkind first: default task, accept exactly task or schedule, and reject\nanything else (or extra arguments) with ''Usage: smrt app worker\n[task|schedule]'' and a non-zero exit before the runtime is resolved, the\nregistration imported, or any worker initialised.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(config): declare the legacy type/defaultModel aliases on AIConfigBlock\n\nThe resolver canonicalises these aliases at runtime and the tests exercise\nthem, but the type did not declare them; type-checking the config tests\n(enabled on main by #3360) caught it after merging main.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* test(cli): type the smrt app test helpers for the test type-check\n\nmain now type-checks the CLI test fixtures (#3359); the smrt app tests\nneeded an explicit ProcessEnv type, a hash helper that accepts strings,\nand optional-method calls on the database handle. Test-only change.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(chat): stop a timed-out assistant-route test from leaking a looping spy\n\nPR #3403 left the merge queue when src/sveltekit.test.ts exhausted its\n2 GB Vitest fork. Cause: on the slower CI runner, the Q1 test''s real-time\nbudget (100 ms between storing a 1 s continuation and taking it) was\nmissed, so the first attempt timed out with its recordClientRequestOutcome\nspy still installed (a timed-out attempt never reaches its finally). On\nthe CI retry, vi.spyOn returned that same spy, the helper captured it as\n\"the original\", and each delegated write called itself again after its\ngate: an unbounded async loop that starved the event loop and filled the\nheap. Reproduced locally with the same signature (Ineffective\nmark-compacts at ~2 GB) by slowing only Q1''s first attempt under CI=true.\n\nSpy helpers now delegate to the real ChatService methods captured once at\nmodule load, afterEach restores every spy (it also runs after a timed-out\nattempt), and Q1 runs on a virtual clock anchored on the continuation''s\nown createdAt, so it no longer depends on runner speed. No route change:\npost-GC heap across the file grows ~0.3 MB per test.\n\nRefs #3368","tree":{"sha":"330291a01785c2f5bbac8ae544e847f6f7cc02c9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/trees/330291a01785c2f5bbac8ae544e847f6f7cc02c9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/happyvertical/smrt/git/commits/0cf612bdf3424f92cc7fcdf09e72e13b8c46fc24","verification":{"payload":"tree 330291a01785c2f5bbac8ae544e847f6f7cc02c9\nparent 64b6065c10087664edc9fcbbe9ad5c91c1f4dae8\nauthor Will Griffin <willgriffin@gmail.com> 1791052817 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791052817 +0000\n\nfeat: starter-driven consumer surface — move framework glue into packages (#3367) (#3403)\n\n* feat(config): declare consumed SMRT packages in smrt.config\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* feat(core): add single Vite preset smrt() with path conventions\n\nComposes the Oxc legacy-decorator config, smrtConsumer and smrtPlugin under\n@happyvertical/smrt-core/vite. The consumer package list comes from the\nsmrt.config consumer.packages declaration or the packages option.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* feat(svelte): add ./app shell, owner setup form and diagnostics WebMCP\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* feat(app-runtime): add canonical application ID and configuration fingerprint\n\nMove resolveApplicationId() and runtimeConfigurationFingerprint() from the\ntemplate''s runtime-identity script into the root entry so the web health route\nand process managers share one byte-compatible implementation.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* feat(app-runtime): add SvelteKit entry exporting handle and init\n\nAdd the @happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit subpath: a runtime factory\nreturning a destructurable { handle, init } that composes the fail-closed\nstartup gate, URL tenant selection (selection only), the signed session with\ntenant context, and authorized-tenant locals; plus mountable health,\ndiagnostics, owner-setup load/actions, and session layout load.\n\nOwner setup re-checks loopback peer and host per request. @sveltejs/kit is an\noptional peer, externalized from the build.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): document the SvelteKit runtime entry\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(app-runtime): export the session tenant authorization rule\n\nExtract the deployed restoreSession() direct/inherited membership check into\nisSessionTenantAuthorized() so the SvelteKit entry applies the same rule.\nBehaviour of restoreSession() is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(app-runtime): verify session tenant, run resolve once, honour RLS isolation\n\nThe SvelteKit entry no longer uses createSessionHandler. Its own session step:\n- accepts a session tenant only when isSessionTenantAuthorized() passes\n  (active direct or legitimately inherited membership); any other session is\n  unauthenticated, and only verified tenants are published as tenantContext;\n- runs downstream code at most once, rethrowing its error unchanged, and\n  fails closed with 500 and cleared locals when the session layer fails;\n- forces postgresRls for database-rls isolation.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): describe verified session tenant and RLS handling\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(app-runtime): keep skip paths and classOptions inside RLS isolation\n\nUnder database-rls isolation a session skip path now skips session loading\nonly and still runs downstream inside the request RLS transaction as an\nanonymous principal. During an RLS request classOptions()/databaseConfig()\nreturn the transaction-bound request database unless the class overrides its\ndb; the session service and tenant selector always use the base config.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* docs(app-runtime): document RLS skip paths and request-scoped classOptions\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(svelte): report MCP App bridge construction failure as state\n\nuseMcpApp constructed the portable bridge inside onMount without a guard,\nso a view opened top-level (no embedding host) or given a non-exact origin\ncrashed the component mount. Surface the failure through the existing\nerror state so views keep their ordinary inline fallback.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-mcp): one-call MCP app route defaults and metadata route\n\nAdd mountMcpAppRoute to ./sveltekit so a consumer''s MCP route is a single\ncall: the allow-list is the app''s explicitly declared models, principals\ncome from the SvelteKit session locals populated by smrt-users\n(principalFromSessionLocals), and every authenticated principal must be a\nhuman with an id, a tenant and every required scope. Application tool and\nresource policies compose with the default and can only narrow it.\n\nmountMcpRoute gains an optional bearer auth source that replaces the\nsession principal when it yields an adapter. mountMcpProtectedResource-\nMetadataRoute serves RFC 9728 metadata only at the advertised path, and\ncreateHostedMcpResourceAuth (./auth) builds the cached hosted adapter from\nSMRT_MCP_* environment. createDefaultMcpAppServer exposes the same\ndefaults from the root entry. The endpoint stays one stateless POST with a\nfresh protocol server per request; no OAuth authorization server is added.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(svelte): ship McpAppsBridge view shell on ./mcp-apps\n\nReplace the template overlay''s McpAppsBridge.svelte with a shipped\ncomponent on the existing @happyvertical/smrt-svelte/mcp-apps subpath. It\nowns one mount-scoped portable bridge, renders app-supplied heading,\ndescription and children, and offers the portable ui/request-display-mode\nonly when the bound host advertises the mode; host absence, refusal or a\nmissing capability keep the view inline with a status. It imports no\nserver code, credentials or OpenAI helpers (the overlay''s\nrequestOpenAiDisplayMode is replaced by the bridge''s own gated request).\n\nStrings use the smrt-svelte i18n catalog and the control is the smrt-ui\nButton. The synthetic-host browser gate covers the advertised, missing and\nrefused modes, top-level fallback, disposal and a 320px viewport.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): point MCP Apps wiring at the shipped route and view defaults\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): validate request Origin on the MCP endpoint\n\nmountMcpAppRoute resolves its principal from the cookie-backed session, so\nanother origin could drive mutating tools as the signed-in user wherever an\napp-wide CORS policy allowed the Mcp-* headers or a same-site sibling still\nreceived the Lax cookie. Streamable HTTP servers must also validate Origin.\n\nAdd checkOrigin/trustedOrigins (names as in the chat assistant routes).\nBefore bearer authentication, principal resolution and any task or tool\ndispatch, a present Origin must parse to the request URL''s own origin or a\nconfigured trusted origin (exact scheme, host, port; null, malformed and\npath-bearing origins refused); without Origin, Sec-Fetch-Site cross-site or\nsame-site is refused. Refusals are HTTP 403 with the safe JSON-RPC error\ndata { code: ''mcp_origin_denied'', retryable: false }. Clients without\nbrowser origin signals, including the smrt-app-cli bridge, are unaffected.\nOn by default for mountMcpAppRoute with an explicit opt-out; additive and\noff unless set for mountMcpRoute. Bearer requests are checked too. The\nmetadata route and deprecated REST mounts are unchanged. Same-origin uses\nevent.url; X-Forwarded-* is never read.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): note the MCP route''s default origin check\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(cli): port app identity, operation lock, writer lease and process primitives\n\nPort the template''s smrt-runtime-identity, smrt-operation-lock,\nsmrt-writer-lease, smrt-process and smrt-provider-readiness scripts into\npackages/cli/src/app with the same state files, record shapes and messages,\nso the CLI and a running application share one lock domain. Storage\nidentity comes from @happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime (new workspace\ndependency). Readiness modules resolve from the application, not the CLI.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(cli): port app export/import portability with filesystem assets\n\nPort smrt-portability.mjs and smrt-portability-assets.mjs unchanged in\nbehaviour: schemaVersion 2 bundles, non-portable secret tables/columns\nemptied, no-replace mode-0600 publish, private regular-file bundle reads,\nexact schema match, empty-target import, parent-first plan, and the asset\nstaging journal with crash recovery. The template''s asset portability\nsuite is ported verbatim against the new modules.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(cli): add smrt app command group for application operations\n\n`smrt app install|setup|recover|start|stop|doctor|open|backup|export|import`\nports scripts/smrt-app.mjs; `migrate`, `worker [task|schedule]`, and\n`dev|build|vite` port smrt-prepare-migration, smrt-worker, smrt-vite and\nsmrt-mcp-apps. JSON output, exit codes, state files and environment\nvariables are unchanged; failures are one secret-redacted stderr envelope\nthat also carries a LocalRuntimeError code (migration_failed). `start`\nspawns bin/smrt-web.mjs so the recorded process identity proof is\nunchanged. `main()` dispatches `app` before manifest/config loading so\nVite arguments pass through verbatim. The primitives are exported without\nside effects from @happyvertical/smrt-cli/app.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): run the built CLI in-process from bin/smrt.js\n\nThe bin wrapper spawned dist/index.js with spawnSync, so a signal sent to\nthe wrapper (a container''s SIGTERM to PID 1) never reached the command and\n`smrt app worker` could not shut down gracefully the way the template''s\ndirectly-run worker script did. Import dist in-process instead; the\nsource/tsx fallback keeps the child process.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* docs(cli): document the smrt app command group\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): serialize stale lock and lease reclamation\n\nDeciding operation.lock/writer.lease is stale and unlinking it were two\nsteps, so a reclaimer paused between them could unlink the live lock a\nfaster reclaimer had just created and admit two owners (review R1; same\nlogic as the template''s smrt-operation-lock/smrt-writer-lease scripts).\nReclaim now runs under an exclusive SQLite transaction on a mutex database\nin the state directory (a kernel lock released when its holder dies) and\nonly removes the exact dead record it re-reads there. Acquisition and the\nrecord formats are unchanged.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): resolve readiness modules with ESM import conditions\n\ncreateRequire().resolve() applied require conditions and rejected\npackages that export only an `import` condition, which the template''s\nin-app import() loaded (review R3). Resolve the application''s installed\npackage through its exports map with node/import/module-sync/default\nconditions and subpath patterns.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): match the web process by exact argv tokens\n\nThe identity check accepted any command line containing `smrt-web.mjs`\nand `--smrt-instance=<nonce>` as substrings, so a recycled pid running\ne.g. evil-smrt-web.mjs or a suffixed nonce could be signalled (review R5;\nsame check as the template''s smrt-process.mjs). Require the final token to\nbe exactly the instance flag and the token before it to have basename\nsmrt-web.mjs, and expose verifyOwnedProcess() for re-checking before a\nsignal. The template launcher path still matches.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): confirm a failed start''s launcher exited before dropping it\n\nOn a readiness failure start sent SIGTERM and immediately removed app.pid,\nso a launcher that ignored SIGTERM kept serving and holding writer.lease\nwith nothing left for `smrt app stop` to find (review R4; same as the\ntemplate''s smrt-app.mjs). Escalate SIGTERM then SIGKILL through the owned\nchild handle, remove the record only after a confirmed exit, and otherwise\nkeep it and name the pid. `stop` now re-verifies the process identity\nimmediately before signalling (review R5).\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): require bundle evidence before recovering an import as complete\n\nA leftover asset journal plus matching per-table row counts was taken as\nproof the import had committed, so count-preserving modified rows were\nreported as a successful import (review R6; same as the template''s\nportability scripts). Record an additive `committedAt` in the journal\nafter the transaction commits, and otherwise accept `complete` only when\nevery table''s rows equal the bundle''s. Journals written by the template\nscripts remain readable and recover through row verification.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(cli): cover review findings R1-R6 and document the changed contracts\n\nDeterministic interleavings for stale reclamation, an import-only\nreadiness package, a launcher that ignores SIGTERM or cannot be killed,\ndeceptive process command lines, count-preserving import recovery, and a\nreal `smrt db:migrate` child with sentinel credentials (R2 counter-evidence).\nExport the new primitives from @happyvertical/smrt-cli/app.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): keep the process record when its identity cannot be re-checked\n\n`stop` treated a failed or empty re-check of the recorded pid''s command\nline as \"not ours\", so a transient ps/PowerShell failure removed app.pid\nand reported success while the writer kept running (review T1). Re-checks\nnow distinguish gone, mismatched (recycled pid), and unverifiable; only the\nfirst two drop the record, and an unverifiable process is neither signalled\nnor forgotten. An empty command line is unverifiable in readOwnedProcess\ntoo.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(cli): resolve readiness packages with Node''s own ESM resolver\n\nThe hand-written exports resolver picked the first inserted pattern key\ninstead of Node''s PATTERN_KEY_COMPARE order and fell through a matched\nnull condition (review T2). Delegate to Node''s default ESM resolver,\nre-parented to the application root through a temporary\nmodule.registerHooks resolve hook (the parent argument of\nimport.meta.resolve is still flagged on Node 26), and keep the\ninstalled-package containment check. resolvePackageEntry is removed.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(cli): cover review findings T1 and T2\n\nA failing second process query during `smrt app stop`, and exports\nfixtures for pattern precedence, matched null targets, condition order,\nexact-before-pattern keys, array fallbacks, and invalid targets.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(chat): store clientRequestId and verified attachments on user messages\n\nChatService.sendMessage gains two optional, additive inputs: a sending\nclient''s clientRequestId (stored in metadata, used as a retry key) and\nalready-verified attachment records (stored in attachments). Both are\nuntrusted data on the actor''s own message, never authority.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* feat(chat): mount the AssistantDock server routes from one call\n\nAdd the @happyvertical/smrt-chat/sveltekit server subpath with\nmountAssistantRoutes(options), returning GET/POST handlers for a single\n[...path] route that serve everything the dock calls: member-scoped thread\nand message reads, thread creation, streamed sends, browser-tool resume,\nhost-backed attachment upload, and data-surface action preview/apply.\n\nThe principal comes from event.locals (smrt-users session handler) or an\ninjected resolver, never from headers or the body. Threads are served only\nfrom the actor''s keyed assistant session room in the active tenant. The AI\nclient is injected; allowedTools is fail-closed and every tool runs under\nexecuteAsPrincipal. Repeated clientRequestIds never store a second message.\n\nThe browser half ships from ./svelte: createAssistantHttpTransport\n(createSmrtAssistantTransport with the writeEndpoint filled in) and\ncreateAssistantHttpActionClient (HTTP AssistantActionClient with #2990\noutcome mapping).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document the mounted assistant routes\n\nDescribe mountAssistantRoutes and its browser half in the AssistantDock\nguide, README and package AGENTS, and close the guide''s gaps 1 and 7\n(no shipped HTTP action client or member-scoped read endpoint).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): reserve client requests on a deterministic message id\n\nA user message sent with a clientRequestId now gets a UUIDv5 primary key\nover tenant, room, thread, actor and clientRequestId (clientRequestMessageId)\nand is inserted, never upserted. The primary key''s uniqueness is the atomic,\ndurable reservation: a second insert, from any replica and at any later\ntime, throws ChatClientRequestConflictError before any stats update. On a\nPostgreSQL transaction handle the insert runs under a savepoint, so a\nconflict does not abort the caller''s transaction.\n\nAdd getThreadMessagesFrom (membership-gated read of a message and what\nfollows it, by id) and recordClientRequestOutcome (the author records how\nthe turn ended in metadata.turnOutcome).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): answer duplicate assistant sends from the database reservation\n\nReplace the per-process in-flight map and the 200-message transcript scan\nwith the deterministic user-message id: a send looks its reservation up by\nid, and a send that loses the insert race on any replica is answered as the\nduplicate. The answer follows the stored send: completed (a reply follows),\ncancelled, in_progress, or 409 turn_failed when its turn failed or it has\nno reply and no outcome after abandonedTurnMs. A retry never re-runs a\nturn. Each turn records its outcome on its send.\n\nThe reservation includes the thread, so one clientRequestId sent to two of\nthe actor''s threads is two sends (review F2).\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): describe the durable send reservation\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): link turn replies to their send and keep it in continuations\n\nrunAssistantTurn takes an originMessageId: every reply and tool message it\nauthors carries replyToMessageId = that id (sendAgentReply gains the\noptional, room/tenant-validated replyToMessageId), and a suspension stores\nit in the continuation. A resume restores it from the consumed\ncontinuation, never from the request, reports it through onResumed and\nin its result. AssistantContinuationStore gains an optional has(); both\nbuilt-in stores implement it.\n\nChatService.getThreadMessageReplies replaces getThreadMessagesFrom: it\nreturns a stored message and the replies linked to it, not what follows it\nin the thread. recordClientRequestOutcome records the continuation a\nsuspended turn waits on and accepts a running outcome.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): settle assistant sends from their own continuation and replies\n\nDuplicate answers read a send''s replies by their replyToMessageId link, so\noverlapping sends in one thread keep their own replies (review M3). A\nsuspended send stays in_progress exactly while its continuation is\nwaiting, regardless of abandonedTurnMs (M1). A resume settles only the send\nrecorded in the continuation it consumes; the request''s clientRequestId is\nignored, and a missing or expired continuation changes no send (M2). A\nresumed leg records running while it works. Tests cover these and a send\nthat fails after its reservation is stored.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document reply links and resume settlement\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): have the turn runner record its own lifecycle, compare-and-set\n\nrunAssistantTurn reports each lifecycle fact through onState at the moment\nit becomes true, whoever reads the events: running when a leg starts (a\nresume once it consumed its continuation), suspended with the continuation\nid once it is stored and before client_tool_calls is emitted, and\ncompleted (after the reply is stored), cancelled or failed when the leg\nends. A resume whose continuation is missing reports nothing. onResumed is\nremoved (it never shipped).\n\nChatService.recordClientRequestOutcome is now monotonic per leg and\ncompare-and-set on the row revision, re-checking after a conflict: a late\nwrite from an earlier leg, a running that does not consume the waiting\ncontinuation, or anything after a terminal outcome is refused. It returns\nwhether the write applied.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): settle assistant sends from the turn, not the HTTP reader\n\nThe routes pump every turn to its end into a buffer the response reads\nfrom, and record outcomes from the runner''s onState, so a reader that\npauses, cancels or leaves early can no longer leave a send unsettled or\noverwrite a resumed leg with a stale suspended. A client disconnect still\ncancels the turn through the request signal, as the dock''s Stop does. A\nlinked assistant reply is completion evidence for duplicate answers even\nwhen the marker is stale.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): document runner-recorded send outcomes and disconnects\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* test(chat): pin forward-only outcome writes on PostgreSQL\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): claim continuations on take until the resume is recorded\n\nThe built-in continuation stores'' take now claims the entry instead of\ndeleting it: no second take returns it (exactly one resume still wins) and\nhas() stays true, so a duplicate-send poll never sees a resuming send as\nsuspended with no continuation. The runner calls the new optional\nrelease() once it has recorded running; an unreleased claim expires after\nCONTINUATION_CLAIM_TTL_MS (60 s). createAssistantTurnResponse gains an\noptional onCancel, called synchronously when the reader cancels the body.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): cancel the detached turn when the response body is cancelled\n\nEach turn runs on an AbortController composed with the request''s signal and\naborted from the response''s onCancel, so a client that leaves cancels the\nturn (a pending server tool call is skipped and the send records\ncancelled) even on adapters that never abort request.signal.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* docs(chat): describe body-cancel disconnects and claimed continuations\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* fix(chat): keep resume claims alive and never run an unrecorded resume\n\nQ1: an actively claimed continuation now lives CONTINUATION_CLAIM_TTL_MS\nfrom its claim, whatever its original lifetime; the original lifetime\napplies only to unclaimed (or lapsed-claim) entries, which become\nclaimable again.\n\nQ2: onState''s result now matters for a resumed leg''s running report: when\nit resolves false or throws, the runner keeps the claim, does not release,\nand stops the leg with resume_not_recorded before any model or tool call.\nThe route returns recordClientRequestOutcome''s result.\n\nRefs #3368\n\n* feat(config): add ai block to the smrt.config schema\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* feat(config): add one AI provider resolver\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* build(chat): depend on smrt-config for the shared AI resolver\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* feat(core,chat,agents): resolve AI providers through smrt-config\n\nChat dev routes, core and agents share the resolver and provider key map;\ncore also honours the smrt.config ai block.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* docs(config): document the AI provider resolver and ai block\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): bind AI credentials to their provider and redact URLs to origin\n\nCredentials follow the provider of their source; displayed base URLs show\nonly the origin; core consumes the full resolved provider when the ai block\nnames one and ignores partial blocks.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): keep AI credential binding across config layers and core paths\n\nMerge ai block layers with provider ownership, build the core client from the\nbound resolver result on every path, and normalise provider/model aliases.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(core): type the defaultModel read in AI resolution\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* fix(config,core): route every AI config merge through one canonicalising helper\n\nFold provider/type and model/defaultModel before merging, drop a lower\nlayer''s credentials when a higher layer names another provider, and set both\nclient aliases from the resolved result.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* refactor(app-runtime): own app identity, state custody, lock, lease, and readiness\n\nThe CLI''s `smrt app` port (#3371) and the SvelteKit runtime entry (#3369)\neach carried an implementation of `resolveApplicationId` and\n`runtimeConfigurationFingerprint`, and the template server could only reach\nthe writer lease and provider-readiness probe through the CLI. Make\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime` the single owner:\n\n- the CLI re-exports app-runtime''s identity and fingerprint (one function,\n  asserted identical, golden vectors unchanged);\n- state root, operation lock, writer lease, stale reclaim, and provider\n  readiness move into app-runtime''s root entry; `@happyvertical/smrt-cli/app`\n  re-exports them unchanged, so a web process takes the same lease without a\n  runtime dependency on the CLI. File names, record formats, and messages are\n  unchanged;\n- their tests (including review findings R1, R3, T2) move with them, because\n  the `node:fs` interleaving hooks cannot reach an externalized dependency.\n\n`runtimeConfigurationFingerprint` now accepts the structural\n`FingerprintRuntime` the CLI already exported (a widening). An explicit\n`SMRT_APP_ID` no longer also requires a readable package name in the CLI,\nmatching what the web process already did.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(smrt-svelte): include MCP Apps bridge strings in the server i18n snapshot\n\n`buildI18nSnapshot` registers smrt-svelte''s catalogs by side-effect import,\nbut the shipped `McpAppsBridge` catalog (`ui.mcp_apps.*`) was never imported\nthere, so a server-built snapshot omitted its three keys and the bridge fell\nback to unresolved defaults on the client.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(smrt-svelte): export the diagnostics WebMCP contract without Svelte\n\nThe runtime diagnostics route lists `smrt.runtime.diagnostics.read` in its\npublic tool inventory, but the tool name was only reachable through the\n`./app` barrel, which loads Svelte components and theme CSS. Add the\nadditive `./app/runtime-diagnostics` subpath for the plain-TypeScript module\nso server routes import the one constant the browser tool registers under.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): honour PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH in the M5 gate\n\nEvery other Playwright config in the repository launches an installed\nChromium when this variable is set; the M5 gate''s did not, so `pnpm test:m5`\ncould not launch a browser on hosts where the downloaded headless shell is\nmissing system libraries (for example `libgbm` on NixOS).\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(core): dedupe @sveltejs/kit in the smrt() Vite preset\n\nSvelteKit recognizes `redirect()` and `fail()` results by class identity.\nA registry install resolves the SMRT SvelteKit entries'' optional\n`@sveltejs/kit` peer from the application, but a linked or workspace package\n(the M5 reference gate, a monorepo app) resolves it from its own directory.\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit`''s owner-setup redirect then\nthrows a foreign `Redirect` and a successful claim renders as a 500. The\npreset now adds `resolve.dedupe: [''@sveltejs/kit'']` through a\n`smrt:shared-runtime` plugin, a no-op for registry installs.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* fix(app-runtime): leave the provider-readiness import to Node at runtime\n\nThe readiness module specifier is operator configuration resolved from the\napplication root when the probe runs. Now that the web server imports the\nprobe, Vite analyzed that dynamic import and warned on every dev start; mark\nit `@vite-ignore` as the resolver import beside it already is.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): configure Vite with the smrt() preset\n\n`vite.config.ts` becomes `plugins: [sveltekit(), smrt()]`. The decorator\ntransform comes from the preset, and the consumed package list moves to\n`smrt.config.ts` `consumer.packages` (profiles, tenancy, users), so it stays\nexplicit and deterministic. Verified against the template''s real\n`smrt.config.ts` at Vite config time and by a full SvelteKit `vite build` in\nthe M5 gate.\n\nRefs #3370\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): mount the SvelteKit runtime from app-runtime\n\nThe server bootstrap the template copied into every app now comes from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-app-runtime/sveltekit`. `src/lib/server/smrt.ts` builds\none `createSmrtSvelteKitRuntime()` with the writer lease and provider\nreadiness probe from app-runtime''s root entry, so the web process no longer\nimports `scripts/` or the CLI. `hooks.server.ts` re-exports its `handle` and\n`init`; `App.Locals` extends `SmrtRuntimeLocals`; health, diagnostics,\nlayout-session, and owner-setup routes mount the package handlers, with the\nonboarding handoff cleanup as the app''s `onOwnerClaimed` hook.\n`getCollection`/`getSmrtConfig` read `runtime.classOptions()` per request.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/server/tenancy.ts`, `application-runtime.ts`,\n`runtime-diagnostics.ts` and their template tests. Their behaviour is\nproven in app-runtime''s `sveltekit.test.ts`. The M5 gate now takes its\n`diagnostics-authorization` case from that suite and keeps exercising the\nmounted route in the browser. `runtimeEntry.test.ts` proves the template\nmounts the runtime and its handlers.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): use the shipped app shell and owner setup UI\n\nThe root layout is `AppShell` from `@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/app` (Provider,\nThemeProvider with its CSS, AdminShell, TenantNav, and the read-only\nruntime-diagnostics WebMCP tool for signed-in sessions); the app supplies\nonly its navigation, title, and tenant label. `/setup` renders\n`OwnerSetupForm` with SvelteKit''s `enhance`, and `/settings` renders\n`ShellSettingsPage`.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/RuntimeDiagnosticsWebMcp.svelte` and\n`src/lib/runtime-diagnostics-webmcp.ts`. The library''s theme CSS imports\nand the `enhance` hook are proven in a real SvelteKit build and browser by\nthe M5 gate (owner onboarding, discovery, lifecycle).\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): run lifecycle operations through smrt app\n\nEvery lifecycle script is now a one-line `smrt app <operation>` call from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-cli`: `dev`, `build`, `preview` (`start`), `db:migrate`\n(`vite build && smrt app migrate`), `app:*`, and the task/schedule workers.\nCompose workers run `pnpm exec smrt app worker task|schedule`, and the\nruntime image no longer copies `scripts/`. `smrt.config.ts` takes its\napplication ID from app-runtime.\n\nDeleted: the whole `template/scripts/` directory (13 files, about 2,990\nlines) and `runtimePortabilityAssets.test.ts`, which the CLI carries verbatim\nin `src/app/__tests__/portability-assets.test.ts`. The M5 gate now takes its\ntwo asset-portability cases from that suite. `runtimeOperations.test.ts`\nasserts behaviour, not script source: every `app:*` script maps onto a real\n`smrt app` operation, the setup recovery sequence names existing scripts,\nand the deployed web/worker topology still holds.\n\nThe M5 harness runs the copied app''s own `smrt app setup` / `smrt app dev`\nand takes identity and state-root values from app-runtime. Its scope and\n`.bin` directories are now real directories of per-entry links, so `pnpm\nbuild` inside the copy (which ships `pnpm-workspace.yaml`) can never relink\nthis checkout''s `node_modules`.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* docs(template-sveltekit): describe the shipped shell and diagnostics tool\n\nThe package guide still described a hand-wired Provider/ThemeProvider/\nAdminShell layout and the template-owned `RuntimeDiagnosticsWebMcp`. Point it\nat `AppShell`, `OwnerSetupForm`, and `ShellSettingsPage` from\n`@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/app`, and at the mounted diagnostics handler.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* refactor(template-sveltekit): mount the MCP Apps overlay on app-mcp defaults\n\nThe opt-in `mcp-apps-template/` overlay is now one route file:\n`mountMcpAppRoute({ models: [Item], requiredScopes: [''items.read''], ... })`\nwith the session principal default, the default-on Origin check, and hosted\nbearer auth from `createHostedMcpResourceAuth` (profile from the runtime,\nthe application-owned `resolveHostedMcpPrincipal` binding). The metadata\nroute is `mountMcpProtectedResourceMetadataRoute`. The workflow tool and the\n`ui://` resource with its OpenAI display metadata stay as app content in the\nroute. `McpAppsBridge` comes from `@happyvertical/smrt-svelte/mcp-apps`.\n\nDeleted: `src/lib/server/mcp.ts`, `mcp-hosted.ts`, `src/lib/McpAppsBridge.svelte`,\nand `mcpHostedCache.test.ts`, whose caching/retry behaviour app-mcp''s\n`auth-hosted.test.ts` covers. `mcpAppsOptIn.test.ts` pins the overlay''s file\nset and options. `pnpm typecheck` now type-checks the overlay against the\ntemplate; app-mcp and mcp-openai are workspace devDependencies for that. The\nsmrt-app-mcp README example no longer imports the deleted\n`application-runtime` module.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): ship a typecheck script in the generated app\n\nStandards §10 requires scaffolded SvelteKit templates to ship `typecheck` as\na TypeScript pass plus `svelte-check`; the template shipped only `check`.\nAdd `svelte-kit sync && tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json && svelte-check`.\nBoth halves pass in a scaffolded copy of this template, the MCP Apps\noverlay included.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* feat(app-mcp): limit MCP catalogs by tool effect\n\nA required scope applies uniformly to every published tool, so a principal\nholding only items.read could discover and invoke a model''s create, update\nand delete tools. Add an effects option to createMcpAppServer (and so to\ncreateDefaultMcpAppServer and mountMcpAppRoute) using the WebMCP vocabulary\nread/write/destructive. mcpToolEffect classifies with the existing read-only\ndetection (canonical readOnlyHint, else the _list/_get name rule); any other\ntool is destructive unless it declares destructiveHint: false. Excluded\ntools are filtered from the catalog itself, so they are absent from\ntools/list and a direct call gets the same unknown-tool error as a\nnonexistent name. Omitting the option keeps today''s catalog.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-mcp): bind bearer principals around MCP dispatch\n\nBearer authentication runs inside mountMcpRoute, after a runtime entry has\nalready opened the request permission context (under database-rls, an RLS\ntransaction from the cookie session, anonymous without one). Add an\nadditive bindPrincipal option (also on mountMcpAppRoute): after bearer\nauthentication the smrtOptions database thunk, task handling and tool\nexecution run inside the application''s binding for that principal, and the\nresponse is materialized before the binding returns. A binder that throws\nbefore dispatch or never runs it answers 403 with the safe\nmcp_tool_access_denied error. The cookie-session path is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* feat(app-runtime): run a route-verified principal in its own permission context\n\nAdd runtime.runAsPrincipal(principal, fn) for principals a route\nauthenticates itself (an MCP bearer token). It resolves the user''s live\npermissions in the principal''s tenant on the base connection, requires an\nauthorized membership, caps the set to the principal''s granted scopes, and\nruns fn in withPrincipalPermissionContext under the same RLS rule as the\nsession step, so databaseConfig()/classOptions() return a transaction that\npublishes that user, tenant and permission set. Application isolation keeps\nthe base connection. Additive; existing request handling is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): keep remote MCP read-only and bind bearer identity\n\nThe MCP Apps overlay gated every Item tool on items.read, exposing create,\nupdate and delete to read-scoped principals, and its bearer calls ran in\nthe anonymous request RLS transaction. Set effects: [''read''] and\nbindPrincipal: runtime.runAsPrincipal. A live PostgreSQL test drives a\ncookie-free bearer request through the real runtime.handle under\ndatabase-rls and asserts the bearer user, tenant and token-capped\npermissions on the transaction, the tenant''s row, precedence over another\nuser''s cookie, fail-closed denial without membership, and unchanged\napplication isolation.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(mcp-apps): describe effect limits and bearer principal binding\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-runtime): export the bound-principal type from the SvelteKit entry\n\nCallers of runtime.runAsPrincipal() could not name the principal type\nbecause only runtime.ts declared it.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): authorize bound bearer calls with live-capped scopes\n\nrunAsPrincipal capped the published permissions to live membership, but\nthe MCP tool policy still authorized from the token''s scopes, so under\napplication isolation a member whose role no longer granted items.read\ncould read through a token that still carried it. runAsPrincipal now hands\nits callback the principal with scopes set to that effective set (additive:\nzero-argument callbacks still work), and the binder''s run() accepts it.\nDispatch authorizes with the token scopes intersected with the bound\nscopes, so a binder can only narrow authority; changing id or tenant, or\nmalformed scopes, is the safe denial. A partially revoked principal keeps\nits remaining tools. Live PostgreSQL tests cover cloud and self-hosted.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): apply effect limits to MCP task lifecycle calls\n\ntasks/get, tasks/update and tasks/cancel checked only task owner and\ntenant, so a read-only route could read, resume or cancel a task created by\na destructive task tool through an unrestricted route sharing the store.\nRecover the task''s originating action from its job row (persisted\nobject_type and method, via the public SmrtJobCollection API, no schema\nchange) and require that the allow-listed generated tool for it passes the\nroute''s effects filter; otherwise answer with unknown-task semantics.\n\ntoolPolicy is not re-applied to lifecycle calls: the job does not record a\ncreating workflow tool, and workflows (the Iolaus conformance reference)\nfront a policy-hidden generated task tool, so doing so broke a documented\npattern. Owner and tenant scoping are unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(app-mcp): document live-capped bearer scopes and task effect checks\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(app-mcp): gate task lifecycle calls on the effective principal\n\nA bearer whose live permissions were revoked (the binder hands back empty\neffective scopes) could still tasks/get, tasks/update (supplying the\nawaited input, so destructive work resumed) and tasks/cancel an existing\ntask: lifecycle calls skip toolPolicy and the effective-scope check ran\nonly for tool calls. Add taskPrincipalPolicy to createMcpAppServer, a\nprincipal-level predicate evaluated before the task store is touched and\nanswered as an unknown task on denial or error. createDefaultMcpAppServer\n(and so mountMcpAppRoute) supplies its principal scope policy — kind, id,\ntenant and every required scope of the effective principal — composed with\nany app predicate, which can only narrow. It needs no originating tool, so\nworkflow-created tasks behind policy-hidden tools stay reachable to an\nauthorized owner. Without the hook a custom server is unchanged.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(app-mcp): document principal gating of task lifecycle calls\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* docs(cli): use the s-m-r-t brand prose in the app command section\n\nThe README check (scripts/check-readmes.mjs) rejects the obsolete\nspelling; the pre-push hook caught it.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(svelte): annotate the owner setup form''s structural native elements\n\nThe strict raw-primitive check flags the native <form> and the hidden\ntoken <input>. Both are deliberate: the form must POST without\nJavaScript, and the hidden input is a non-interactive carrier for the\nsingle-use bootstrap token. Annotate them with the documented\nraw-primitive-allow reason instead of wrapping them in primitives.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(svelte): document AppShell props and use the emitted font-family token\n\nThe pre-push jsdoc check requires one sentence per exported prop in a\nstrict package, and the typography/token checks reject\n--smrt-typography-body-font-family, which no theme emits; the themes\nemit --smrt-font-family.\n\nRefs #3374\n\n* fix(svelte): use the emitted spacing token in McpAppsBridge\n\n--smrt-space-3 is not emitted by any theme; the emitted token is\n--smrt-spacing-3. Caught by the pre-push svelte-token check.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* test(template-sveltekit): run the bearer RLS fixture under the CI role model\n\nIn CI the PostgreSQL suites connect as the unprivileged smrt_ci role,\nwhich owns the fixture table it creates, so FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY\nrefused the fixture''s own seed INSERT (42501); locally it passed only as a\nsuperuser. Seed the rows before enabling RLS, confine all committed DDL\n(users tables, policy helpers, RLS table and policy) to a fixture schema\nreached through the connection search_path and dropped on teardown, and\ndrop the login role without DROP OWNED BY (smrt_ci administers but lacks\nthe privileges of roles it creates). Assert the application connects as a\nNOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS role that sees no rows without a principal. No\nsuperuser URL is used.\n\nRefs #3373\n\n* fix(template-sveltekit): skip only an absent generated register module\n\nThe registration fallback swallowed every ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND (and any\nerror whose message mentioned smrt-register.js), so a missing dependency\nimported by an existing generated module let startup continue without\npackage-qualified registration. Skip the import only when the error''s\nmissing module (Node''s url, or \"Cannot find module ''<path>''\" from Node and\nVite''s module runner) is the generated register module itself; rethrow\neverything else. Tests evaluate the shipped predicate against real Node and\nVite errors for both cases.\n\nRefs #3369\n\n* fix(cli): accept only task or schedule for smrt app worker\n\nrunWorker branched only on ''schedule'', so a misspelled kind such as\n''scheduel'' was reported as that kind but started the task worker. Parse the\nkind first: default task, accept exactly task or schedule, and reject\nanything else (or extra arguments) with ''Usage: smrt app worker\n[task|schedule]'' and a non-zero exit before the runtime is resolved, the\nregistration imported, or any worker initialised.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* fix(config): declare the legacy type/defaultModel aliases on AIConfigBlock\n\nThe resolver canonicalises these aliases at runtime and the tests exercise\nthem, but the type did not declare them; type-checking the config tests\n(enabled on main by #3360) caught it after merging main.\n\nRefs #3372\n\n* test(cli): type the smrt app test helpers for the test type-check\n\nmain now type-checks the CLI test fixtures (#3359); the smrt app tests\nneeded an explicit ProcessEnv type, a hash helper that accepts strings,\nand optional-method calls on the database handle. Test-only change.\n\nRefs #3371\n\n* test(chat): stop a timed-out assistant-route test from leaking a looping spy\n\nPR #3403 left the merge queue when src/sveltekit.test.ts exhausted its\n2 GB Vitest fork. Cause: on the slower CI runner, the Q1 test''s real-time\nbudget (100 ms between storing a 1 s continuation and taking it) was\nmissed, so the first attempt timed out with its recordClientRequestOutcome\nspy still installed (a timed-out attempt never reaches its finally). On\nthe CI retry, vi.spyOn returned that same spy, the helper captured it as\n\"the original\", and each delegated write called itself again after its\ngate: an unbounded async loop that starved the event loop and filled the\nheap. Reproduced locally with the same signature (Ineffective\nmark-compacts at ~2 GB) by slowing only Q1''s first attempt under CI=true.\n\nSpy helpers now delegate to the real ChatService methods captured once at\nmodule load, afterEach restores every spy (it also runs after a timed-out\nattempt), and Q1 runs on a virtual clock anchored on the continuation''s\nown createdAt, so it no longer depends on runner speed. No route change:\npost-GC heap across the file grows ~0.3 MB per test.\n\nRefs #3368","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwUwhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAArgYQAGHt7qMhixAWFUVijOzo00kA\ns6Xdyk6ZRe7fT8bljS6+yEdHZjtuU+F0rj+tP9o1pMhAWZadqJdwNYj4j1e4MYZO\nOQIV74xxJauMVFXUdeOrYwZtFkPVadaUpsUFevJ0ezpzG+RJsz0mxIp2sszg9u/D\nGutETO4oMdABquF6lZb6N4BcJujfg+wnpptV9nlpP4DX4edus+6UbhMSss27XIBw\nFW5cxur6PeCChLQ1xAKr+0prROWWtoueby7nhSQQ3vYRTUGBGOneWkUMp4AaYLTT\n6/jlRDMmiPwPZoExywvF/HNGLZxS4Riv7